#!/usr/bin/env bash
# heimdall-caveman — hmd-native output-compression level owner.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS
# Output compression ("caveman mode") used to depend entirely on the external
# `caveman` Claude Code plugin (https://github.com/juliusbrussee/caveman):
# hmd could only REPORT the plugin's live level (bin/heimdall-caveman-block)
# for its own agent-spawn preambles, never OWN the level or the rule text,
# and had nothing of its own if that plugin were ever absent. Per owner
# directive (2026-08-29/30, delta-brief brief-1788088676-56663): hmd now owns
# the level get/set and the per-level rule text directly, so output
# compression is no longer a hard dependency on that external plugin. See
# CLAUDE.md "Token Efficiency" for the ownership-transfer note this requires
# there, and bin/heimdall-caveman-block / bin/heimdall-caveman-compliance for
# the two adjacent, UNCHANGED tools this one does not replace (the former
# still reports the plugin's own live level for spawn preambles; the latter
# is still the read-back auditor, reused — not duplicated — below).
#
# SCOPE: ONE settable, operator-facing level — ultra. Collapsed from three
# (lite, full, ultra) on 2026-09-01 per owner directive: hmd's audience is
# technical, already established at setup by the "are you a coder?" question,
# so the gentler tiers (lite, full) have no audience of their own. `get`,
# `set`, the stored state file, and fresh-install default are all ultra-only
# now — `is_valid_level` accepts nothing else. `set lite` / `set full` are
# still ACCEPTED (never a hard error) and mapped forward to ultra with a
# stderr notice, matching this file's existing fail-open posture elsewhere:
# a legacy level name behaves like any other out-of-scope input this tool
# already resolves forward instead of wedging on.
#
# `rules lite` and `rules full` remain directly callable BY EXPLICIT NAME
# ONLY (never reachable through get/set/default/migration — see
# `_is_renderable_level` vs. `is_valid_level` below). This is a deliberate,
# narrow carve-out, not a partial revert: bin/heimdall-caveman-eval's
# existing three-arm comparison (hmd_lite/hmd_full/hmd_ultra vs
# upstream_skill) already spent real, non-trivial API quota producing its
# committed snapshot, and this task was explicitly told not to trigger a
# `--confirm-spend` refresh. Ripping the two arms out would either orphan
# that snapshot or force a refresh to replace it — neither is this task's
# call to make. Neither name is an operator-facing level any more; `--help`
# and every settable/gettable path name ultra only. Flagged here for the
# owner to correct if this reads as scope creep the other way.
#
# The plugin's other modes (off, the wenyan-* classical-Chinese variants, and
# the independent commit/review/compress modes) remain out of scope, exactly
# as before this change.
#
# LICENCE PROVENANCE
# The caveman plugin (github.com/juliusbrussee/caveman) is MIT-licensed,
# Copyright (c) 2026 Julius Brussee (that repo's root LICENSE file, byte-
# identical to the copy bundled in the installed plugin cache). A separate,
# scoped Business Source License 1.1 carve-out (that repo's LICENSING.md)
# covers only its "Engine-linked" directories — engine/, proxy/,
# cacheengine/, rewriter/, browse/, mcp/, shrink/, the cavemem Go core, and
# shared/platform/ — none of which the caveman SKILL/hook content reused
# below lives under; skills/caveman/SKILL.md and the two hook scripts it
# informs are plain MIT. MIT permits vendoring with attribution, which is
# what the `_rules_*` functions below do: their prose is adapted from that
# repo's skills/caveman/SKILL.md (the "## Rules" / "## Intensity" / example
# sections), reformatted per level and with two deliberate, disclosed
# changes from the source text — see "DISCLOSED ADAPTATIONS" below. The
# MIT copyright notice above is preserved here per the license's "included
# in all copies or substantial portions" condition. The hook-wiring SHAPE
# (SessionStart injects the full per-level rules once; UserPromptSubmit
# reinforces just the level name every turn) is REIMPLEMENTED FROM OBSERVED
# BEHAVIOR of that repo's hooks/caveman-activate.js and
# hooks/caveman-mode-tracker.js — no line of shell code below is copied from
# either JS file.
#
# DISCLOSED ADAPTATIONS (so nobody mistakes these for copy errors)
#   1. The source SKILL.md's "## Rules" section states "Drop: articles"
#      unconditionally, directly under an "## Intensity" table whose own
#      `lite` row says "Keep articles + full sentences" — a real self-
#      contradiction in the source (the installed hook passes "## Rules"
#      through unfiltered for every level, so the live plugin actually ships
#      this contradiction at level lite). Reimplemented here as level-aware
#      instead: the `lite` block below keeps articles, matching what the
#      table — the unambiguous source of truth — actually specifies.
#   2. "Switch: `/caveman lite|full|ultra`" is adapted to point at THIS
#      tool's own `set` subcommand instead of the plugin's slash command,
#      since that command may not be installed once this tool owns the
#      level.
#
# THE HONEST LIMIT (read before trusting this as enforcement)
# Moving level-ownership in-house changes WHO OWNS THE RULES, not WHETHER
# THEY ARE FOLLOWED. A SessionStart/UserPromptSubmit hook can only INJECT
# instruction text before the model replies — it cannot rewrite, veto, or
# even see the model's actual output. Measured on this repo's own session
# transcript while the EXTERNAL plugin was active at level `full`: 3.25% of
# assistant prose characters were still caveman-targeted filler (see
# bin/heimdall-caveman-compliance, wired as `hmd caveman-audit`). In-housing
# level ownership does NOT fix that gap and this file makes no claim that it
# does. Run `hmd caveman-audit <session.jsonl>` for the measured read-back;
# this tool never gates on it, only points at it (`rules` output does too).
#
# PARITY IMPROVEMENTS OVER THE PLUGIN (owner directive, 2026-08-29/30 --
# "check if you can improve hmd's version of caveman further to do more than
# [the plugin]"). The plugin only ever INJECTS instruction text -- it has no
# access to hmd's own audit history, so it structurally cannot do the first
# thing below. Two things were built; two were evaluated and rejected. All
# four are recorded here so the reasoning survives, not just the code.
#
# BUILT 1 -- measure/act loop (the strongest candidate; only hmd can do this,
# because only hmd owns both the level and the audit). bin/heimdall-caveman-
# compliance used to be pure report-and-forget: run it, read the number,
# nothing downstream ever used it again. It now also persists its last
# GENUINE (non-degraded, non-zero-prose) measurement to
# $HEIMDALL_HOME/caveman-compliance-last.json (best-effort, fail-open; see
# its own _persist_last()). `rules` below reads that file back
# (_last_measurement_summary, symlink-refused and size-capped the same way
# _safe_read_level is) and, when a real measurement exists, appends the
# ACTUAL last-measured filler floor and its top offending categories to the
# injected rule text, instead of the generic "audit via `hmd caveman-audit`"
# pointer alone. This turns a static instruction into one grounded in this
# session's own history -- concrete and self-correcting rather than generic.
# It is still not enforcement -- "THE HONEST LIMIT" above fully applies here
# too -- it just makes the instruction specific instead of generic. Silent
# (no section appended) when no measurement has ever been taken, or the file
# is missing/corrupt/symlinked; this never fabricates a number, and the
# phrasing always says "floor", never "savings" -- same discipline
# heimdall-caveman-compliance's own human-readable output already holds
# itself to.
#
# RETIRED 2026-09-01 -- `diff` preview. `heimdall-caveman diff <lvl> [<base>]`
# used to unified-diff one level's rule text against another, so an operator
# could see what changing levels would change before running `set`. Its only
# non-degenerate use was comparing two DIFFERENT real levels; the ultra-only
# collapse above (see "SCOPE") means every remaining call would diff ultra
# against ultra -- a command whose one useful case no longer exists. Dropped
# rather than kept as permanent dead weight; had zero callers outside this
# file's own tests (verified: no other bin/, hooks/, skills/, or evals/ file
# invoked it) before removal.
#
# REJECTED 1 -- mechanical per-surface enforcement (e.g. making "never
# compress a commit message" a code guarantee instead of a rule of prose).
# Rejected as a placebo: the only mechanism available anywhere in this stack
# is a SessionStart/UserPromptSubmit hook injecting text before the model
# replies (see "THE HONEST LIMIT" above); nothing in this tool's reach can
# inspect, veto, or rewrite what the model then actually writes into a commit
# message, a PR body, or a security warning. The one carve-out that IS
# already mechanical rather than advisory -- heimdall-caveman-compliance
# stripping fenced/inline code before counting filler -- operates at AUDIT
# time, not generation time, and already existed before this change; it is
# reused here, not duplicated. Claiming more than that for a text-injection
# hook would be exactly the false "enforcement" framing this task warned
# against.
#
# REJECTED 2 -- auto-escalation (raise the level automatically as the
# session's context fills). Rejected on the merits, not just on scope: the
# causal story runs in both directions -- a filling context is also exactly
# when a hard problem most needs the nuance compression would strip, so
# "compress more when full" is not obviously an improvement over "compress
# less when it matters most". There is also no context-pressure signal
# available inside this single-purpose level-owner without reaching into
# another subsystem (e.g. heimdall-ctx-meter) for a benefit that has never
# been measured, and the trigger would have to live in a hook this change is
# explicitly barred from touching (hooks/hooks.json is owned by a sibling
# change in flight right now). Shipping it would require exactly the kind of
# unmeasured efficacy claim this task said not to make. Left undone.
#
# STORAGE
# hmd's own level: $HEIMDALL_HOME/caveman-level (one line, ultra-only as of
# 2026-09-01 -- see "SCOPE" above). NOT the plugin's
# ${CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR:-$HOME/.claude}/.caveman-active file — that file is
# read exactly once, as a migration SEED, only when hmd's own state file
# does not exist yet AND the plugin's flag already holds "ultra" literally —
# a plugin flag of lite/full is now out of scope for seeding (same bucket as
# wenyan-*/off below) and falls through to DEFAULT_LEVEL exactly like any
# other value this tool does not own. After that first read, hmd never
# ADOPTS a value from the plugin's file again, and never writes to it — but
# every `get` DOES read it once more, read-only, purely to compare (using a
# wider lite/full/ultra check than the seed check above — see "DIVERGENCE /
# RESTART SAFETY" below for why).
#
# DIVERGENCE / RESTART SAFETY
# Directly observed on this repo's own session (owner report, 2026-08-30):
# the plugin silently overwrites its OWN flag file back to its default
# ("full") on session restart, with no warning. Verified first-hand -- write
# "ultra" to the plugin's .caveman-active at 17:05, restart the session
# (/login), and the file's mtime advances to 17:18:07 holding "full", not
# "ultra" -- nobody touched it. An operator who set ultra therefore gets
# full back with no notice, and a compliance measurement taken afterward
# would silently be scored against the wrong level. hmd's own state file is
# immune (a different path the plugin never writes to), but immunity alone
# leaves an operator staring at two disagreeing files with no signal about
# which one is real. So `get` (and therefore `rules` with no explicit level,
# since it resolves through `get`) compares hmd's stored value against the
# plugin's LIVE flag on every call, not just at migration, and prints a
# one-line warning to stderr -- never stdout, so a hook's injected text
# stays clean -- when they disagree. hmd's value always wins; this is a
# visibility fix, not a new source of truth. This comparison deliberately
# uses a WIDER lite/full/ultra check than `set`/migration's ultra-only gate
# (see _is_renderable_level vs is_valid_level below): hmd can only ever
# resolve to "ultra" now, but the plugin, if still installed, can still
# independently report lite or full, and that mismatch is exactly the
# "operator staring at two disagreeing tools" case this warning exists for.
# A plugin flag outside lite/full/ultra (wenyan-*, off, garbage) stays
# silent, same as always. See _warn_if_diverged() below.
#
# SUBCOMMANDS
#   heimdall-caveman get                  print the current level (migrates
#                                         from the plugin's flag once if
#                                         unset and the flag already says
#                                         "ultra"; on every call also compares
#                                         against the plugin's LIVE flag and
#                                         warns on stderr, never stdout, if
#                                         they disagree -- hmd's value always
#                                         wins; falls back to "ultra" — the
#                                         only level this tool has — if no
#                                         valid value is stored)
#   heimdall-caveman set <lvl>            persist ultra as current; a legacy
#                                         lite/full value is ACCEPTED and
#                                         mapped to ultra with a stderr
#                                         notice, never a hard error -- an
#                                         unrecognized value still is
#   heimdall-caveman rules [<lvl>]        print the rule text for <lvl> (or
#                                         the current level if omitted);
#                                         appends the last MEASURED compliance
#                                         figure when `hmd caveman-audit` has
#                                         ever recorded one -- see "PARITY
#                                         IMPROVEMENTS OVER THE PLUGIN" above.
#                                         Explicit `rules lite` / `rules full`
#                                         still render (see "SCOPE" above) —
#                                         eval-harness compatibility only,
#                                         not an operator-facing level
#   heimdall-caveman hooks-json           print the exact SessionStart +
#                                         UserPromptSubmit hooks.json
#                                         fragments needed to wire this tool
#                                         (reference text only — this tool
#                                         never edits hooks.json itself)
#   heimdall-caveman path                 print the path to hmd's state file
#   heimdall-caveman -h | --help          usage
#
# Fails open throughout: a missing/corrupt state file, a missing $HOME, or a
# missing plugin flag never crashes a session start — `get` always prints a
# valid level.

set -uo pipefail

HEIMDALL_HOME="${HEIMDALL_HOME:-${HOME:-/tmp}/.heimdall}"
STATE_FILE="$HEIMDALL_HOME/caveman-level"
PLUGIN_FLAG="${CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR:-$HOME/.claude}/.caveman-active"
# VALID_LEVELS gates what can be SET, STORED, MIGRATED-to, or reported by
# `get` -- ultra-only as of 2026-09-01 (see "SCOPE" above). RENDERABLE_LEVELS
# is the wider set `rules <lvl>` and the divergence check accept, by explicit
# name / plugin-flag comparison only -- see _is_renderable_level below.
VALID_LEVELS="ultra"
RENDERABLE_LEVELS="lite full ultra"
DEFAULT_LEVEL="ultra"
MAX_FLAG_BYTES=64
# Written by bin/heimdall-caveman-compliance's _persist_last() after every
# GENUINE (non-degraded) measurement; read back by _last_measurement_summary
# below. See "PARITY IMPROVEMENTS OVER THE PLUGIN" in the header comment.
LAST_MEASURE_FILE="$HEIMDALL_HOME/caveman-compliance-last.json"
LAST_MEASURE_BYTES_CAP=16384

is_valid_level() {
  case " $VALID_LEVELS " in
    *" $1 "*) return 0 ;;
    *) return 1 ;;
  esac
}

# Wider than is_valid_level: lite/full/ultra, not ultra-only. Gates two
# things ONLY -- explicit `rules <lvl>` and the divergence check's read of
# the plugin's (separate, still three-valued) flag -- never what hmd itself
# can be SET to, STORE, or MIGRATE to. See "SCOPE" above.
_is_renderable_level() {
  case " $RENDERABLE_LEVELS " in
    *" $1 "*) return 0 ;;
    *) return 1 ;;
  esac
}

# Symlink-safe, size-capped read. Mirrors the threat model the vendored
# source's own caveman-config.js readFlag() hardens against (a local writer
# to the state dir swapping the file for a symlink so whatever later prints
# the "level" leaks unrelated file content) — same posture, reimplemented in
# bash rather than copied. Returns nothing (empty stdout) on any anomaly:
# missing file, symlink, or oversized.
#
# Deliberately does NOT gate on is_valid_level (it did, pre-2026-09-01
# collapse, when there was only one notion of "valid"). Callers now need two
# different notions of valid from the SAME two files this reads (hmd's own
# state file and the plugin's flag) -- ultra-only for what hmd can be set to
# or migrate to, lite/full/ultra for what the divergence check and explicit
# `rules <lvl>` accept -- so validity is each caller's own decision now
# (is_valid_level or _is_renderable_level), made explicitly after the read.
_safe_read_level() {
  local f="$1"
  [ -n "$f" ] || return 0
  [ -e "$f" ] || return 0
  [ -L "$f" ] && return 0
  [ -f "$f" ] || return 0
  head -c "$MAX_FLAG_BYTES" "$f" 2>/dev/null | tr -d '[:space:]' | tr '[:upper:]' '[:lower:]'
  return 0
}

# Same symlink-safe, size-capped read posture as _safe_read_level above,
# generalized to arbitrary bytes instead of a validated lite/full/ultra token
# -- this one backs LAST_MEASURE_FILE, which holds a JSON blob, not a level.
# Returns empty stdout on any anomaly: missing, symlink, or unreadable.
_safe_cat_capped() {
  local f="$1" cap="$2"
  [ -n "$f" ] || return 0
  [ -e "$f" ] || return 0
  [ -L "$f" ] && return 0
  [ -f "$f" ] || return 0
  head -c "$cap" "$f" 2>/dev/null
  return 0
}

# Symlink-safe atomic write: refuses a symlinked parent dir or an existing
# symlink at the target path, writes to a mktemp file in the SAME directory
# (guarantees same filesystem so `mv` is atomic) with 0600 perms, then
# renames into place.
_safe_write_level() {
  local f="$1" val="$2" dir tmp
  dir="$(dirname "$f")"
  mkdir -p "$dir" 2>/dev/null || return 1
  [ -L "$dir" ] && return 1
  if [ -e "$f" ] && [ -L "$f" ]; then return 1; fi
  tmp="$(mktemp "$dir/.caveman-level.tmp.XXXXXX" 2>/dev/null)" || return 1
  if ! printf '%s\n' "$val" > "$tmp" 2>/dev/null; then
    rm -f "$tmp"
    return 1
  fi
  chmod 600 "$tmp" 2>/dev/null || true
  if ! mv -f "$tmp" "$f" 2>/dev/null; then
    rm -f "$tmp"
    return 1
  fi
  return 0
}

# One-time migration: only fires when hmd's own state file does not exist
# yet. READS (never writes) the plugin's flag; seeds hmd's state only if
# that value is "ultra" -- the only level this tool still owns as of the
# 2026-09-01 collapse (see "SCOPE" above). Silent no-op otherwise —
# `get`'s own fallback to $DEFAULT_LEVEL covers every other case (plugin
# absent, plugin flag unset, plugin flag holds a now-out-of-scope lite/full
# value, or an always-out-of-scope mode like a wenyan-* variant or "off").
_migrate_once() {
  [ -f "$STATE_FILE" ] && return 0
  local seed
  seed="$(_safe_read_level "$PLUGIN_FLAG")"
  is_valid_level "$seed" || return 0
  _safe_write_level "$STATE_FILE" "$seed" || true
  return 0
}

# Runs on every `get`, not just at migration -- the plugin has been observed
# to silently rewrite its OWN flag file back to its default on session
# restart (see "DIVERGENCE / RESTART SAFETY" above), so a one-time migration
# read cannot catch a disagreement that appears later. NEVER changes the
# resolved value; only decides whether to print a heads-up. Uses
# _is_renderable_level (lite/full/ultra), deliberately WIDER than
# is_valid_level (ultra-only): hmd itself can only ever resolve to "ultra"
# now, but the plugin, if still installed, can still independently report
# lite or full, and that is exactly the disagreement this warning exists to
# surface -- narrowing this to ultra-only would silently stop reporting the
# one divergence this mechanism was built for. Silent (no output) when the
# plugin flag is absent or holds a value outside lite/full/ultra (wenyan-*,
# off, garbage) -- that is not a disagreement about a level this tool owns.
_warn_if_diverged() {
  local resolved="$1" plugin_val
  plugin_val="$(_safe_read_level "$PLUGIN_FLAG")"
  _is_renderable_level "$plugin_val" || return 0
  [ "$plugin_val" = "$resolved" ] && return 0
  echo "heimdall-caveman: level diverged -- hmd stores '$resolved', plugin flag now reports '$plugin_val'; hmd's value wins (plugin file is compared on every get, never re-adopted after first migration)" >&2
}

# Reads back bin/heimdall-caveman-compliance's last persisted GENUINE
# measurement (LAST_MEASURE_FILE) and renders it as an appendable rule-text
# section, or prints nothing at all. Fails open at every step: no python3,
# no file, a symlinked file, an oversized file, invalid JSON, a non-dict
# payload, or a missing/wrong-typed field all resolve to empty output --
# never a stack trace, never partial/garbled text reaching stdout. The JSON
# payload is passed as a single argv element (sys.argv[1]), never
# interpolated into the python source, so file content can never become code.
_last_measurement_summary() {
  command -v python3 >/dev/null 2>&1 || return 0
  local raw
  raw="$(_safe_cat_capped "$LAST_MEASURE_FILE" "$LAST_MEASURE_BYTES_CAP")"
  [ -n "$raw" ] || return 0
  python3 -c '
import json, os, sys, time

try:
    rec = json.loads(sys.argv[1])
except Exception:
    sys.exit(0)
if not isinstance(rec, dict):
    sys.exit(0)

share = rec.get("filler_share_of_prose_chars_pct")
prose_chars = rec.get("prose_chars")
counts = rec.get("filler_counts")
measured_at = rec.get("measured_at")

if not isinstance(share, (int, float)):
    sys.exit(0)
if not isinstance(prose_chars, (int, float)) or prose_chars <= 0:
    sys.exit(0)
if not isinstance(counts, dict) or not counts:
    sys.exit(0)

top = sorted(
    ((k, v) for k, v in counts.items() if isinstance(v, (int, float))),
    key=lambda kv: (-kv[1], kv[0]),
)[:3]
if not top:
    sys.exit(0)
top_str = ", ".join("%s %d" % (k, int(v)) for k, v in top)

age = ""
if isinstance(measured_at, (int, float)):
    mins = max(0, int((time.time() - measured_at) / 60))
    age = " (%dm ago)" % mins

session = os.path.basename(str(rec.get("session_path") or "unknown session"))

print("## Last Measured Compliance")
print()
print(
    "Last audit%s on %s: filler floor %.2f%% of %d prose chars (measured, "
    "not a savings figure). Top offenders: %s. Refresh: "
    "`hmd caveman-audit <session.jsonl>`."
    % (age, session, share, int(prose_chars), top_str)
)
' "$raw" 2>/dev/null
}

cmd_get() {
  _migrate_once
  local lvl
  lvl="$(_safe_read_level "$STATE_FILE")"
  is_valid_level "$lvl" || lvl="$DEFAULT_LEVEL"
  _warn_if_diverged "$lvl"
  printf '%s\n' "$lvl"
}

cmd_set() {
  local lvl="${1:-}"
  if [ -z "$lvl" ]; then
    echo "error: heimdall-caveman set needs a level (ultra)" >&2
    return 2
  fi
  lvl="$(printf '%s' "$lvl" | tr '[:upper:]' '[:lower:]')"
  # lite/full are RETIRED, not unknown -- accept and map forward instead of
  # hard-erroring, same fail-open posture as everywhere else in this file
  # (a legacy value resolves forward; it never wedges the tool). An actually
  # unrecognized value (e.g. "bogus") still hard-errors below, unchanged.
  case "$lvl" in
    lite|full)
      echo "heimdall-caveman: '$lvl' is retired -- hmd runs a single level now (ultra). Storing 'ultra' instead." >&2
      lvl="ultra"
      ;;
  esac
  if ! is_valid_level "$lvl"; then
    echo "error: invalid level '$lvl' -- must be one of: $VALID_LEVELS" >&2
    return 2
  fi
  if _safe_write_level "$STATE_FILE" "$lvl"; then
    printf 'heimdall-caveman: level set to %s (%s)\n' "$lvl" "$STATE_FILE"
    return 0
  fi
  echo "error: could not write $STATE_FILE" >&2
  return 1
}

cmd_path() {
  printf '%s\n' "$STATE_FILE"
}

# --- per-level rule text -----------------------------------------------
# Pre-computed per level rather than parsed from markdown at request time —
# same justification bin/heimdall-caveman-compliance's own FILLER_PATTERNS
# constant documents: this content is small and stable, so a markdown-
# parsing dependency would buy nothing here. See "DISCLOSED ADAPTATIONS"
# above the shebang header for the two places this deliberately departs
# from the vendored source's literal wording.

_rules_lite() {
  cat <<'RULES'
HMD OUTPUT COMPRESSION — level: lite (rules injected below; not a compliance guarantee — audit via `hmd caveman-audit`)

Respond tight. All technical substance stay. Only fluff die.

## Persistence

ACTIVE EVERY RESPONSE. No revert after many turns. No filler drift. Still active if unsure. Off only: "stop caveman" / "normal mode".

Default level: full. Change level: `heimdall-caveman set lite|full|ultra`.

## Rules

Drop: filler (just/really/basically/actually/simply), pleasantries (sure/certainly/of course/happy to), hedging. Keep articles (a/an/the) and full sentences — professional but tight. Short synonyms (big not extensive, fix not "implement a solution for"). Technical terms exact. Code blocks unchanged. Errors quoted exact. Answer only what the question asked: skip extra examples, product names, failure-mode asides, or code samples the question did not request.

Pattern: [thing] [action] [reason]. [next step].

Not: "Sure! I'd be happy to help you with that. The issue you're experiencing is likely caused by..."
Yes (full-level phrasing shown for contrast; lite itself keeps articles/full sentences): "Bug in auth middleware. Token expiry check use `<` not `<=`. Fix:"

## This level (lite)

No filler/hedging. Keep articles + full sentences. Professional but tight.

Example — "Why React component re-render?"
lite: "Your component re-renders because you create a new object reference each render. Wrap it in `useMemo`."

Example — "Explain database connection pooling."
lite: "Connection pooling reuses open connections instead of creating new ones per request. Avoids repeated handshake overhead."

## Auto-Clarity

Drop compression for: security warnings, irreversible action confirmations, multi-step sequences where fragment order risks misread, user asks to clarify or repeats question. Resume after the clear part is done.

## Boundaries

Code/commits/PRs: write normal. "stop caveman" or "normal mode": revert. Level persists until changed or session end.
RULES
}

_rules_full() {
  cat <<'RULES'
HMD OUTPUT COMPRESSION — level: full (rules injected below; not a compliance guarantee — audit via `hmd caveman-audit`)

Respond terse like smart caveman. All technical substance stay. Only fluff die.

## Persistence

ACTIVE EVERY RESPONSE. No revert after many turns. No filler drift. Still active if unsure. Off only: "stop caveman" / "normal mode".

Default level: full. Change level: `heimdall-caveman set lite|full|ultra`.

## Rules

Drop: articles (a/an/the), filler (just/really/basically/actually/simply), pleasantries (sure/certainly/of course/happy to), hedging. Fragments OK. Short synonyms (big not extensive, fix not "implement a solution for"). Technical terms exact. Code blocks unchanged. Errors quoted exact. Answer only what's asked: skip extra examples, product names, failure-mode asides, or code samples not requested.

Pattern: [thing] [action] [reason]. [next step].

Not: "Sure! I'd be happy to help you with that. The issue you're experiencing is likely caused by..."
Yes: "Bug in auth middleware. Token expiry check use `<` not `<=`. Fix:"

## This level (full)

Drop articles, fragments OK, short synonyms. Classic caveman.

Example — "Why React component re-render?"
full: "New object ref each render. Inline object prop = new ref = re-render. Wrap in `useMemo`."

Example — "Explain database connection pooling."
full: "Pool reuse open DB connections. No new connection per request. Skip handshake overhead."

## Auto-Clarity

Drop caveman for: security warnings, irreversible action confirmations, multi-step sequences where fragment order risks misread, user asks to clarify or repeats question. Resume caveman after clear part done.

## Boundaries

Code/commits/PRs: write normal. "stop caveman" or "normal mode": revert. Level persists until changed or session end.
RULES
}

_rules_ultra() {
  cat <<'RULES'
HMD OUTPUT COMPRESSION — level: ultra (rules injected below; not a compliance guarantee — audit via `hmd caveman-audit`)

Respond max terse. All technical substance stay. Only fluff die.

## Persistence

ACTIVE EVERY RESPONSE. No revert after many turns. No filler drift. Still active if unsure. Off only: "stop caveman" / "normal mode".

Default level: ultra (the only level). Change level: `heimdall-caveman set ultra`.

## Rules

Drop: articles (a/an/the), filler (just/really/basically/actually/simply), pleasantries (sure/certainly/of course/happy to), hedging, conjunctions. Fragments OK. Abbreviate (DB/auth/config/req/res/fn/impl). Arrows for causality (X → Y). One word when one word is enough. Technical terms exact. Code blocks unchanged. Errors quoted exact. Answer only what's asked: skip unrequested examples/names/failure-mode asides/code samples.

Pattern: [thing] [action] [reason]. [next step].

Not: "Sure! I'd be happy to help you with that. The issue you're experiencing is likely caused by..."
Yes: "Bug in auth middleware. Token expiry check use `<` not `<=`. Fix:"

## This level (ultra)

Abbreviate (DB/auth/config/req/res/fn/impl), strip conjunctions, arrows for causality (X → Y), one word when one word is enough.

Example — "Why React component re-render?"
ultra: "Inline obj prop → new ref → re-render. `useMemo`."

Example — "Explain database connection pooling."
ultra: "Pool = reuse DB conn. Skip handshake → fast under load."

## Auto-Clarity

Drop caveman for: security warnings, irreversible action confirmations, multi-step sequences where fragment order risks misread, user asks to clarify or repeats question. Resume caveman after clear part done.

## Boundaries

Code/commits/PRs: write normal. "stop caveman" or "normal mode": revert. Level persists until changed or session end.
RULES
}

# Single dispatch point from a validated level name to its static rule text
# -- used by cmd_rules for both the current/default level and an explicit
# lite/full/ultra argument (see "SCOPE" above for why lite/full still render
# on request), so nothing else in this file can drift from what a level's
# text actually is. The fallback matters: every real caller already validates
# via _is_renderable_level first, so `*)` should be unreachable in practice,
# but if it is ever reached anyway, it must resolve to hmd's actual current
# default (ultra) -- resolving to the RETIRED default (full, pre-2026-09-01)
# would silently reintroduce the exact false-declaration bug this whole file
# exists to prevent.
_rules_text_for_level() {
  case "$1" in
    lite) _rules_lite ;;
    full) _rules_full ;;
    ultra) _rules_ultra ;;
    *) _rules_ultra ;;
  esac
}

cmd_rules() {
  local lvl="${1:-}"
  if [ -z "$lvl" ]; then
    lvl="$(cmd_get)"
  else
    lvl="$(printf '%s' "$lvl" | tr '[:upper:]' '[:lower:]')"
    # Wider than is_valid_level on purpose -- explicit `rules lite`/`rules
    # full` still render (eval-harness compatibility, see "SCOPE" above)
    # even though neither is settable/gettable as hmd's own level any more.
    if ! _is_renderable_level "$lvl"; then
      echo "error: invalid level '$lvl' -- must be one of: $RENDERABLE_LEVELS" >&2
      return 2
    fi
  fi
  _rules_text_for_level "$lvl"
  local last
  last="$(_last_measurement_summary)"
  [ -n "$last" ] && printf '\n%s\n' "$last"
  return 0
}

cmd_hooks_json() {
  cat <<'HOOKSJSON'
hmd does NOT edit hooks/hooks.json itself (siblings own that file right now).
The two entries below are what SessionStart and UserPromptSubmit need to wire
this tool in, in the same style already used by this repo's hooks.json
(PLUGIN root resolved via ${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT} with a git-toplevel fallback,
guarded by [ -x ]).

--- SessionStart (append as one more object in the "SessionStart" array) ---
{
  "hooks": [
    {
      "type": "command",
      "command": "PLUGIN=\"${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT:-$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null || pwd)}\"; CAV=\"$PLUGIN/bin/heimdall-caveman\"; if [ -x \"$CAV\" ]; then \"$CAV\" rules; fi; exit 0"
    }
  ]
}

--- UserPromptSubmit (append as one more object in the "UserPromptSubmit" array) ---
{
  "hooks": [
    {
      "type": "command",
      "command": "PLUGIN=\"${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT:-$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null || pwd)}\"; CAV=\"$PLUGIN/bin/heimdall-caveman\"; if [ -x \"$CAV\" ]; then LVL=\"$(\"$CAV\" get 2>/dev/null)\"; if [ -n \"$LVL\" ]; then printf '{\"hookSpecificOutput\":{\"hookEventName\":\"UserPromptSubmit\",\"additionalContext\":\"HMD OUTPUT COMPRESSION - level: %s. Apply this level'\"'\"'s rules (heimdall-caveman rules). Code/commits/security: write normal.\"}}' \"$LVL\"; fi; fi; exit 0"
    }
  ]
}

stderr is intentionally left UNSUPPRESSED on the SessionStart `rules` call
above (the older draft of this fragment redirected it to /dev/null): `get`
and `rules` write a one-line warning to stderr, never stdout, when hmd's
stored level disagrees with the plugin's live flag (see this tool's own
"DIVERGENCE / RESTART SAFETY" header) -- letting that reach the hook's own
stderr, where an operator or Claude Code's own hook logs can see it, is the
entire point; suppressing it would silently defeat the warning at exactly
the "on startup" moment it matters most. The UserPromptSubmit fragment
deliberately KEEPS `2>/dev/null` on its inner `"$CAV" get` call: without it,
the same warning would repeat on every single user turn for the rest of the
session once a divergence exists, which is worse than useless (warning
fatigue drowns the one signal that matters) -- once per session, at
SessionStart, is the right cadence, matching this fragment's own established
pattern of a full per-session dump versus a cheap per-turn reinforcement.

SessionStart prints the full per-level rules ONCE per session (matches the
plugin's own two-tier design: full dump at session start, short reinforcement
per turn). UserPromptSubmit re-states only the level name each turn, cheaply,
so long sessions do not drift once the SessionStart context gets trimmed —
same rationale as the plugin's own caveman-mode-tracker.js per-turn
reinforcement. Neither entry is added by this tool; a human or the siblings
who own hooks/hooks.json right now must paste these in.
HOOKSJSON
}

usage() {
  cat <<USAGE
Usage: heimdall-caveman <command> [args]

Commands:
  get                 Print the current level (ultra).
  set <lvl>           Set the current level (ultra). A legacy lite/full
                      value is accepted and mapped to ultra with a stderr
                      notice, never a hard error.
  rules [<lvl>]       Print the rule text for <lvl> (default: current level).
                      Appends the last measured compliance figure when one
                      has been recorded (see \`hmd caveman-audit\`). Explicit
                      \`rules lite\`/\`rules full\` still render for
                      eval-harness compatibility -- neither is an
                      operator-facing level any more.
  hooks-json          Print the hooks.json fragments needed to wire this tool.
  path                Print the path to hmd's own state file.
  -h, --help          Show this help.

Storage: $STATE_FILE (hmd-owned; migrates once from the caveman plugin's flag
file if present and hmd has no state of its own yet). Every \`get\` also
compares hmd's stored level against the plugin's LIVE flag and warns on
stderr -- never stdout -- if they disagree; hmd's value always wins. The
plugin has been observed to silently reset its own flag on session restart.

Honest limit: this tool INJECTS instruction text; it cannot verify or enforce
that a model followed it. Run \`hmd caveman-audit <session.jsonl>\` to measure
actual compliance after the fact.
USAGE
}

main() {
  local cmd="${1:-}"
  case "$cmd" in
    get) cmd_get ;;
    set) shift; cmd_set "${1:-}" ;;
    rules) shift; cmd_rules "${1:-}" ;;
    hooks-json) cmd_hooks_json ;;
    path) cmd_path ;;
    -h|--help) usage ;;
    "")
      usage >&2
      return 2
      ;;
    *)
      echo "error: unknown command '$cmd'" >&2
      usage >&2
      return 2
      ;;
  esac
}

main "$@"
